00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:07.230
METE ATAMEL: Hello, my
name is Mete Atamel.

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:09.220
I'm a developer
advocate at Google,

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:12.330
and today I want to go
through a code lab with you.

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:14.700
The name of the
code lab is build

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:18.580
event-driven orchestration
with Eventarc and workflows.

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:20.700
Now, as you can see,
there's two key phrases

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.890
in the title, event-driven
and orchestration.

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:25.920
So let's zoom into
those a little bit

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.540
before we dive into the codelab.

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:31.510
When you have multiple
services, at some point

00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:33.010
these services
need to communicate

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:36.310
and you need to decide
how they will communicate.

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:40.670
One easy way is services to
call each other directly.

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:43.960
So you can have service
A call service B. Service

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:46.390
B can call service C,
and so on and so forth.

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:51.190
This is easy to implement, but
it creates unnecessary coupling

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:52.640
between services.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:54.430
So if you need to
change service B,

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:57.933
you need to know that
service A is calling it

00:00:57.933 --> 00:00:59.350
and you need to
know that it calls

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:02.650
service C, so you need to
think about those dependencies.

00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:06.370
Now, to make it more loosely
coupled, what you can do

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:09.790
is you can use an
event-driven approach.

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:14.410
In an event-driven architecture,
services, they do their work,

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:17.770
and when they are finished,
they just emit an event.

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:19.810
That even goes to
a message broker,

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:22.180
and the message
worker figures out

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:24.730
which other services
care about this message

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:25.810
and it passes it on.

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:27.790
And then the next
service does its work,

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:30.160
and it will just
emit another message.

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:32.890
In this event-driven
model, services

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:34.390
don't know about
each other and they

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:35.680
don't care about each other.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:37.930
Instead, they just
consume messages

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:39.400
and they emit messages.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:43.240
This allows more loosely
coupled kind of architecture,

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:46.000
and it makes it easier
to change services

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.970
without having to think about
the next services in the chain.

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:51.400
At the same time,
it also makes it

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:54.920
hard to trace your architecture.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:56.630
So if something goes
wrong, for example,

00:01:56.630 --> 00:02:00.520
if an event cannot be processed
or if the service goes down,

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:03.670
it will be difficult to figure
out exactly where things are

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:04.960
not working.

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:07.870
Now, a third approach
is called orchestration.

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:10.780
In orchestration, you
have an external service,

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:13.300
an orchestrator,
where you define

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:16.570
how the services should
be called, in what order,

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:19.240
and the orchestrator is
responsible for making sure

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:23.500
that the services are called and
then the result is being taken

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:27.274
and that result is passed
to the next service.

00:02:27.274 --> 00:02:31.600
Now, in orchestration, you
basically bring some order

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:35.030
to your service to
service communication,

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:39.140
and it makes it easier to
trace if something goes wrong.

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:42.760
Now, in this code lab, we are
combining both approaches.

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:46.390
We are combining event-driven,
and we are also combining

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:48.020
orchestration with it.

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:52.150
And the reason for that
is that in some use cases,

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:55.430
you want the structure
provided by orchestration,

00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:57.693
so you want certain
services to be orchestrated

00:02:57.693 --> 00:02:59.110
because you want
to make sure they

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:00.900
are called in a certain order.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:03.400
But at the same time, you don't
want your whole architecture

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:05.440
to be an orchestration.

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:09.370
Instead, you can have the
orchestration be triggered

00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:11.680
by an event, in a
loosely coupled way,

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:15.580
or you can also have the
orchestration send an event

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:17.950
at the end of the orchestration.

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:20.320
This allows the best
of both worlds, where

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:22.660
you have some
things orchestrated

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:25.840
but the other orchestrations
are communicating

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:27.282
in a loosely coupled way.

00:03:27.282 --> 00:03:29.740
So that's what we're going to
build today in this code lab,

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:35.270
and let's just go ahead and
get started with the code lab.

