check for safety.

00:38:08.950 --> 00:38:12.010
If it's not safe, we finish.

00:38:12.010 --> 00:38:13.600
But if it's safe,
we continue, right?

00:38:13.600 --> 00:38:16.270
So it's very useful
to kind of visualize

00:38:16.270 --> 00:38:19.070
what's happening in workflow.

00:38:19.070 --> 00:38:20.980
All right, so our
orchestration, let's

00:38:20.980 --> 00:38:23.020
go back to architecture
review again.

00:38:23.020 --> 00:38:28.480
So we are basically here where
we deployed the services.

00:38:28.480 --> 00:38:31.360
We put them together
into an orchestration.

00:38:31.360 --> 00:38:35.830
And the last step is to actually
connect this orchestration

00:38:35.830 --> 00:38:38.170
to an event from Cloud Storage.

00:38:38.170 --> 00:38:42.070
And we'll do that using
Eventarc Cloud Storage trigger.

00:38:42.070 --> 00:38:46.070
So it will-- that Eventarc will
be used to make the connection.

00:38:46.070 --> 00:38:49.300
So that's step 10.

00:38:49.300 --> 00:38:51.670
Now, in Eventarc,
there's one time setup.

00:38:51.670 --> 00:38:52.840
So you need to--

00:38:52.840 --> 00:38:56.880
first of all, you need
have a service account

00:38:56.880 --> 00:39:01.810
that has Eventarc
event receiver role.

00:39:01.810 --> 00:39:03.520
And you'll use this
service account

00:39:03.520 --> 00:39:05.260
to when you create a trigger.

00:39:05.260 --> 00:39:07.390
You need to have
a service account.

00:39:07.390 --> 00:39:08.390
You have two choices.

00:39:08.390 --> 00:39:10.810
You can actually create
a service account

00:39:10.810 --> 00:39:13.900
and give it to this
Eventarc receiver role,

00:39:13.900 --> 00:39:17.830
or you can use the default
compute service account that

00:39:17.830 --> 00:39:20.260
comes with every project,
and just make sure

00:39:20.260 --> 00:39:21.440
that it has this role.

00:39:21.440 --> 00:39:26.470
So in this case, I'm
just using the default

00:39:26.470 --> 00:39:27.970
compute service
account, so first,

00:39:27.970 --> 00:39:30.670
let's get the project
number because you need that

00:39:30.670 --> 00:39:32.570
for the service account name.

00:39:32.570 --> 00:39:35.650
So let me make this bigger.

00:39:35.650 --> 00:39:38.320
Let's clear this.

00:39:38.320 --> 00:39:40.900
So let's get the project number.

00:39:40.900 --> 00:39:45.770
And then here, we are
adding a policy binding

00:39:45.770 --> 00:39:47.770
and we are just saying
that this service account

00:39:47.770 --> 00:39:50.270
with this project number, that
-compute, this is the default

00:39:50.270 --> 00:39:53.860
compute service account, will
have the role, Eventarc event

00:39:53.860 --> 00:39:54.430
receiver.

00:39:54.430 --> 00:39:56.860
So we are making sure
that it has this role.

00:39:56.860 --> 00:39:59.950
And since we are using
Cloud Storage trigger here,

00:39:59.950 --> 00:40:02.638
we also need to make sure
that the Cloud Storage

00:40:02.638 --> 00:40:04.930
service account, there's also
a default service account

00:40:04.930 --> 00:40:11.170
for that has pubsub publisher
role for it, as well, OK?

00:40:11.170 --> 00:40:13.570
And so I'll just, again,
copy and paste this.

00:40:13.570 --> 00:40:21.250
This is the default service
account for Cloud Storage,

00:40:21.250 --> 00:40:28.370
and then let's make sure that we
have a policy binding for that.

00:40:28.370 --> 00:40:30.340
And it has pubsub
publisher role.

00:40:30.340 --> 00:40:31.640
Now, this is a one time setup.

00:40:31.640 --> 00:40:33.190
So once you do this
once in your project,

00:40:33.190 --> 00:40:34.450
you don't have to do it again.

00:40:34.450 --> 00:40:35.992
But since we are
using a new project,

00:40:35.992 --> 00:40:39.290
I want to make sure that
we have this set up.

00:40:39.290 --> 00:40:44.200
OK, now that we have that, how
do we connect Cloud Storage

00:40:44.200 --> 00:40:46.150
events to workflows?

00:40:46.150 --> 00:40:50.950
Well, we do that through what's
called an Eventarc trigger.

00:40:50.950 --> 00:40:53.620
So let me copy and
paste this, and then I

00:40:53.620 --> 00:40:54.805
will explain what this does.

00:40:57.830 --> 00:41:01.450
So this creates a trigger,
and let's look at the details,

00:41:01.450 --> 00:41:02.260
basically.

00:41:02.260 --> 00:41:03.940
So we are saying
that I want to create

00:41:03.940 --> 00:41:08.350
a trigger with
this name, which is

00:41:08.350 --> 00:41:10.720
going to be trigger, dash,
my workflow name, image

00:41:10.720 --> 00:41:12.550
processing.

00:41:12.550 --> 00:41:13.550
By the way, it's failed.

00:41:13.550 --> 00:41:15.008
So let's-- sometimes
it just fails.

00:41:15.008 --> 00:41:18.340
Let me run this one more
time, see what happens.

00:41:21.280 --> 00:41:24.400
OK, so now it's
creating the trigger.

00:41:24.400 --> 00:41:26.080
So we are saying that
we want to create

00:41:26.080 --> 00:41:29.140
a trigger in this region,
which is US central.

00:41:29.140 --> 00:41:34.640
And I want these events to go
to a workflow with this name,

00:41:34.640 --> 00:41:36.580
image processing,
and this workflow

00:41:36.580 --> 00:41:39.055
using this location, US
central, because everything

00:41:39.055 --> 00:41:40.180
is localizing Google Cloud.

00:41:40.180 --> 00:41:42.520
So that's why we need
to specify the region.

00:41:42.520 --> 00:41:44.600
And then we also have
some event filters.

00:41:44.600 --> 00:41:46.930
So we don't want all the
events to go to workflow.

00:41:46.930 --> 00:41:49.520
We only want events
from this bucket,

00:41:49.520 --> 00:41:55.330
which is the input bucket,
to our workflow input bucket

00:41:55.330 --> 00:41:56.600
that we are interested in.

00:41:56.600 --> 00:42:00.820
And then we also don't want all
the events from this bucket.

00:42:00.820 --> 00:42:02.960
We only want object
finalize events.

00:42:02.960 --> 00:42:05.050
So this is the object
finalize event,

00:42:05.050 --> 00:42:07.850
which gets triggered when
you save a new object.

00:42:07.850 --> 00:42:10.060
So if someone
saves a new object,

00:42:10.060 --> 00:42:11.470
you want that to trigger.

00:42:11.470 --> 00:42:14.183
But otherwise, we don't
care about other events.

00:42:14.183 --> 00:42:15.850
And we also pass in
the service account.

00:42:15.850 --> 00:42:19.777
So this created the trigger
after the second try.

00:42:19.777 --> 00:42:21.610
I don't know what
happened in the first one.

00:42:21.610 --> 00:42:28.800
But if you go to Eventarc,
we should see the trigger,

00:42:28.800 --> 00:42:31.830
see trigger image processing,
and destination is a workflow.

00:42:31.830 --> 00:42:36.120
And it's actually targeting
image processing workflow, OK?

00:42:36.120 --> 00:42:38.850
So everything seems
to be working--

00:42:38.850 --> 00:42:41.310
well, not working,
but at least set up.

00:42:41.310 --> 00:42:45.930
So in step 11, let's just
test out whether this works.

00:42:45.930 --> 00:42:49.350
And to test it out, all
we need to do is deploy--

00:42:49.350 --> 00:42:52.193
well, upload a picture and
see that it's processed.

00:42:52.193 --> 00:42:53.610
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:42:53.610 --> 00:42:57.870
can go to-- we can use
gsutil to upload an image,

00:42:57.870 --> 00:42:59.470
but let's do it from the UI.

00:42:59.470 --> 00:43:04.980
So I go to Cloud Storage,
and I see my input and output

00:43:04.980 --> 00:43:05.530
buckets.

00:43:05.530 --> 00:43:07.890
So let's go to input bucket.

00:43:07.890 --> 00:43:09.660
You see there is no images here.

00:43:09.660 --> 00:43:14.550
I will upload a
picture of myself.

00:43:14.550 --> 00:43:19.050
And now, as this is uploading,
let's actually go to workflows

00:43:19.050 --> 00:43:20.470
and see what happens.

00:43:20.470 --> 00:43:23.760
So if you go to workflows,
click on the workflow,

00:43:23.760 --> 00:43:24.690
image processing.

00:43:24.690 --> 00:43:26.430
You see that there's
an execution now

00:43:26.430 --> 00:43:28.050
that's active, right?

00:43:28.050 --> 00:43:30.840
So the uploading of the
image triggered an event

00:43:30.840 --> 00:43:33.480
through Eventarc, and that
event is now in workflows

00:43:33.480 --> 00:43:36.130
and the workflow is now
working through that event.

00:43:36.130 --> 00:43:40.405
And if you click
in the execution--

00:43:40.405 --> 00:43:42.280
and it's already succeeded
at it, by the way,

00:43:42.280 --> 00:43:44.470
so it's pretty quick.

00:43:44.470 --> 00:43:49.590
But if you look at the inputs
and outputs of the execution,

00:43:49.590 --> 00:43:53.778
you see that this is the
input that it receives.

00:43:53.778 --> 00:43:56.070
So this is the event that it
received from Google Cloud

00:43:56.070 --> 00:43:58.680
Storage, with the bucket
and data and the information

00:43:58.680 --> 00:43:59.730
about the image.

00:43:59.730 --> 00:44:02.070
And this is the output
that workflows did.

00:44:02.070 --> 00:44:04.190
So the output is what
we kind of produced,

00:44:04.190 --> 00:44:06.690
filter service, label service,
resize service, and watermark

00:44:06.690 --> 00:44:08.970
service, they all
returned 200, HTTP 200,

00:44:08.970 --> 00:44:10.900
meaning they all worked fine.

00:44:10.900 --> 00:44:12.582
So it seems like
things are working.

00:44:12.582 --> 00:44:14.040
And if you look at
the logs, we see

00:44:14.040 --> 00:44:19.510
that the event is being
logged, and also the safety,

00:44:19.510 --> 00:44:22.950
whether the image is
safe or not is logged.

00:44:22.950 --> 00:44:28.570
And if we-- let's do one more
image, just for the fun of it.

00:44:28.570 --> 00:44:34.780
Let's upload a beach, a
more interesting picture

00:44:34.780 --> 00:44:38.642
with sunshine and things.

00:44:38.642 --> 00:44:40.850
So let's upload that, and
this will trigger, as well.

00:44:40.850 --> 00:44:43.210
And then just to check
that things are working,

00:44:43.210 --> 00:44:46.510
if we go to this
output bucket, you

00:44:46.510 --> 00:44:49.660
see that we have
three different files.

00:44:49.660 --> 00:44:53.210
So first, Atamel--
so this was my image.

00:44:53.210 --> 00:44:56.110
So we have a labels.txt.

00:44:56.110 --> 00:45:02.500
And if we look at
this labels.txt,

00:45:02.500 --> 00:45:08.620
these are all the labels that
was picked up by Vision API.

00:45:08.620 --> 00:45:14.690
So it says forehead, cheek,
smiles, so that sounds like me.

00:45:14.690 --> 00:45:18.770
Then if you look at 400x400
PNG, that's the resize image.

00:45:18.770 --> 00:45:21.190
So you see that it's squeezed
a little bit, maybe not

00:45:21.190 --> 00:45:25.330
the right way of resizing,
but it's definitely resized.

00:45:25.330 --> 00:45:28.090
And then if you look at
the 400x400 watermark,

00:45:28.090 --> 00:45:31.810
you see that it's the resize
image with the top three

00:45:31.810 --> 00:45:33.940
labels added as a watermark.

00:45:33.940 --> 00:45:36.340
So it seems like
everything is working.

00:45:36.340 --> 00:45:40.810
And then the other picture, the
beach one is more interesting.

00:45:40.810 --> 00:45:42.970
You see that it says
water, cloud, sky, again,

00:45:42.970 --> 00:45:45.290
the same kind of
thing from there.

00:45:45.290 --> 00:45:47.440
So this concludes our code lab.

00:45:47.440 --> 00:45:50.470
You've seen how to combine
an event-driven and an

00:45:50.470 --> 00:45:52.720
orchestrated
architecture into one.

00:45:52.720 --> 00:45:54.490
Hopefully, this
was useful to you.

00:45:54.490 --> 00:45:56.350
Feel free to reach
out to me on Twitter

00:45:56.350 --> 00:45:58.610
if you have any
questions or feedback.

00:45:58.610 --> 00:46:00.160
Thanks very much.

